# {"question": "Who narrates the story, and whose life is introduced at the beginning?", "keywords": ["Harry Potter", "opening"], "reference_answer": "The story is told by a third-person narrator and begins by introducing the Dursleys before focusing on Harry Potter.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "What is Vernon Dursley's occupation?", "keywords": ["Vernon Dursley", "Grunnings"], "reference_answer": "He is the director of Grunnings, a company that makes drills.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Who is Petunia Dursley in relation to Harry?", "keywords": ["Petunia", "aunt", "family"], "reference_answer": "Petunia is Harry's aunt, his mother Lily's sister.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What is the name of the Dursleys' son?", "keywords": ["Dursleys", "son", "Dudley"], "reference_answer": "Their son is Dudley Dursley.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "What is the name of the school Harry is invited to attend?", "keywords": ["school", "Hogwarts"], "reference_answer": "He is invited to attend Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry.", "source_chapter": 4, "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "Who delivers Harry to the Dursleys as a baby?", "keywords": ["Harry", "baby"], "reference_answer": "Albus Dumbledore places baby Harry on the Dursleys' doorstep after Hagrid brings him to Privet Drive.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What distinctive mark is on Harry's forehead?", "keywords": ["scar", "forehead", "lightning"], "reference_answer": "He has a thin scar shaped like a lightning bolt.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "What is the name of the giant who becomes one of Harry's first trusted adult friends?", "keywords": ["giant", "friend", "Hagrid"], "reference_answer": "Rubeus Hagrid, the Hogwarts gamekeeper and Keeper of Keys and Grounds, becomes one of Harry's first trusted adult friends.", "source_chapter": 4, "category": "direct_fact"}
{"question": "Who is the headmaster of Hogwarts?", "keywords": ["headmaster", "Hogwarts", "Dumbledore"], "reference_answer": "Albus Dumbledore is the headmaster of Hogwarts.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "Which Hogwarts house does Harry join?", "keywords": ["Sorting Hat", "house", "Gryffindor"], "reference_answer": "Harry is sorted into Gryffindor.", "source_chapter": 7, "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "Who becomes Harry's closest school friends during his first year?", "keywords": ["friends", "Ron", "Hermione"], "reference_answer": "Ron Weasley and Hermione Granger become Harry's closest friends during his first year.", "source_chapter": [6, 7], "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "What position does Harry play on the Gryffindor Quidditch team?", "keywords": ["Quidditch", "position", "Seeker"], "reference_answer": "Harry plays Seeker, the position responsible for catching the Golden Snitch.", "source_chapter": 11, "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "What object is hidden inside Hogwarts that Voldemort seeks?", "keywords": ["object", "Voldemort"], "reference_answer": "The Philosopher's Stone, which can produce the Elixir of Life and turn metal into gold.", "source_chapter": [13, 17], "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "Who is revealed to be helping Voldemort pursue the Stone?", "keywords": ["Voldemort", "Quirrell"], "reference_answer": "Professor Quirrell is revealed to be helping Voldemort, who is attached to the back of Quirrell's head.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "What protects Harry from Quirrell when they confront each other?", "keywords": ["Harry", "Quirrell", "protection"], "reference_answer": "When Harry touches Quirrell, Quirrell's skin burns and crumbles. Harry's protection is connected to the love and sacrifice of his mother.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "direct_fact"}
# {"question": "How are Harry and Dudley related?", "keywords": ["Harry", "Dudley"], "reference_answer": "They are cousins; Dudley is the son of Harry's aunt Petunia and uncle Vernon.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "character_relationship"}
{"question": "What is the relationship between Harry and Ron?", "keywords": ["Harry", "Ron", "friendship"], "reference_answer": "They become close friends after meeting on the Hogwarts Express and sharing experiences at school.", "source_chapter": [6, 7], "category": "character_relationship"}
{"question": "How does Hermione's relationship with Harry and Ron change?", "keywords": ["Hermione", "Harry", "Ron", "friendship"], "reference_answer": "At first Harry and Ron find Hermione irritating, but after they help rescue her from a troll, the three become close friends.", "source_chapter": 10, "category": "character_relationship"}
{"question": "What connects Harry to Lily and James Potter?", "keywords": ["Harry", "Lily", "James", "parents"], "reference_answer": "Lily and James are Harry's parents. They were killed by Voldemort when Harry was a baby, and Lily's sacrifice protects Harry.", "source_chapter": [1, 17], "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "How does Hagrid regard Harry?", "keywords": ["Hagrid", "Harry"], "reference_answer": "Hagrid is affectionate and protective toward Harry, treating him with warmth and helping him enter the wizarding world.", "source_chapter": [4, 5], "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "What is Draco Malfoy's relationship with Harry at Hogwarts?", "keywords": ["Draco", "Harry"], "reference_answer": "Draco becomes Harry's rival. He looks down on Harry's friends and repeatedly antagonizes Harry and the other Gryffindors.", "source_chapter": [5, 6, 7], "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "How does Professor McGonagall relate to Harry's development as a student?", "keywords": ["McGonagall", "Harry", "teacher"], "reference_answer": "McGonagall is one of Harry's teachers and the head of Gryffindor House. She recognizes his flying ability and helps him join the Quidditch team.", "source_chapter": [7, 11], "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "What is the relationship between Dumbledore and Harry?", "keywords": ["Dumbledore", "Harry"], "reference_answer": "Dumbledore is Harry's headmaster and a protective mentor who understands the importance of his past and explains the Stone's fate to him.", "source_chapter": [1, 17], "category": "character_relationship"}
{"question": "How do Fred and George relate to Harry during his first year?", "keywords": ["Fred", "George", "Harry", "Weasley"], "reference_answer": "Harry's fellow Gryffindors Fred and George Weasley welcome him into the social life of their house and are supportive, playful older students.", "source_chapter": [6, 7, 11], "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "What is the relationship between Neville and Harry?", "keywords": ["Neville", "Harry", "Gryffindor"], "reference_answer": "Neville is Harry's fellow Gryffindor. Although often nervous and underestimated, he becomes part of the group and eventually shows courage by trying to stop his friends from breaking rules.", "source_chapter": [7, 16], "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "How does Snape treat Harry, and how does Harry interpret it?", "keywords": ["Snape", "Harry", "suspicion"], "reference_answer": "Snape treats Harry harshly in class, and Harry interprets his hostility as evidence that Snape may be trying to steal the Stone.", "source_chapter": [8, 11, 13], "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "What connects Hagrid to the dragon Norbert?", "keywords": ["Hagrid", "Norbert", "dragon"], "reference_answer": "Hagrid secretly obtains a dragon egg and raises the hatchling Norbert, becoming emotionally attached even though keeping a dragon is dangerous and against the rules.", "source_chapter": 14, "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "What is the relationship between Quirrell and Voldemort?", "keywords": ["Quirrell", "Voldemort", "servant"], "reference_answer": "Quirrell serves Voldemort and helps him try to obtain the Philosopher's Stone; Voldemort is physically attached to the back of Quirrell's head.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "How do the Weasleys influence Harry's sense of belonging?", "keywords": ["Weasleys", "Harry", "belonging"], "reference_answer": "The Weasleys treat Harry with kindness and include him in their family life, offering warmth and acceptance unlike the Dursleys.", "source_chapter": [6, 12], "category": "character_relationship"}
# {"question": "How does Harry's relationship with the Dursleys contrast with his relationship with the Hogwarts staff?", "keywords": ["Dursleys", "Hogwarts", "care"], "reference_answer": "The Dursleys belittle and isolate Harry, while adults such as Hagrid, McGonagall, and Dumbledore recognize his needs and help him discover his identity and abilities.", "source_chapter": [2, 4, 5, 7], "category": "character_relationship"}
{"question": "How do Harry, Ron, and Hermione combine their strengths to investigate the Stone?", "keywords": ["Harry", "Ron", "Hermione", "Stone"], "reference_answer": "Harry contributes courage and decisive action, Ron contributes wizarding knowledge and strategic thinking, and Hermione contributes research, logic, and practical spellwork. Their cooperation helps them overcome the protections guarding the Stone.", "source_chapter": [13, 16, 17], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "Why does Harry suspect Snape, and what does the final revelation show about that suspicion?", "keywords": ["Snape", "suspicion", "Quirrell"], "reference_answer": "Harry interprets Snape's hostility and behavior during Quidditch as signs that Snape wants the Stone. The final confrontation reveals that Quirrell, not Snape, is serving Voldemort, showing that Harry's evidence led him to the wrong conclusion.", "source_chapter": [11, 13, 17], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How does Harry's childhood with the Dursleys help explain his reaction to the wizarding world?", "keywords": ["Dursleys", "childhood"], "reference_answer": "The Dursleys deny Harry affection, information about his parents, and a sense of belonging. The acceptance and wonder he encounters in the wizarding world therefore feel especially meaningful to him.", "source_chapter": [2, 3, 4, 5], "category": "multi_hop"}
{"question": "How does the information about Harry's parents connect to the protection he experiences at the end?", "keywords": ["parents", "Lily", "sacrifice", "protection"], "reference_answer": "Harry learns that Voldemort killed his parents while trying to kill him as a baby. Dumbledore later explains that Lily's loving sacrifice left Harry with protection that makes it dangerous for Voldemort and Quirrell to touch him.", "source_chapter": [1, 17], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How does Hagrid's trust in Harry connect to the discovery of the dragon and the trio's later troubles?", "keywords": ["Hagrid", "dragon", "Norbert"], "reference_answer": "Hagrid shares the secret of Norbert with Harry and his friends. Their involvement in helping move the dragon exposes them to rule-breaking and punishment, demonstrating how Hagrid's trust and their loyalty can also draw them into risky situations.", "source_chapter": [14, 15], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How does Harry's Quidditch talent affect his place at Hogwarts and his later actions?", "keywords": ["Quidditch", "Seeker", "Harry"], "reference_answer": "His exceptional flying earns him a place on the Gryffindor team, giving him recognition and a strong sense of belonging. His experience with the Snitch and the broom incident also contributes to his suspicion that someone is trying to harm him or interfere with the Stone.", "source_chapter": [9, 11, 13], "category": "multi_hop"}
{"question": "How does the Mirror of Erised help explain Harry's emotional needs?", "keywords": ["Mirror of Erised", "parents", "desire", "Harry"], "reference_answer": "The mirror shows Harry with his deceased parents and extended family, revealing that his deepest desire is to know and be with the family he lost. This contrasts with his neglected life at the Dursleys' home.", "source_chapter": 12, "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "Why does Dumbledore move the Stone out of Gringotts, and how does that connect to the protections at Hogwarts?", "keywords": ["Gringotts", "Stone", "Hogwarts"], "reference_answer": "After the attempted break-in at Gringotts, the Stone is transferred to Hogwarts and protected by a series of challenges created by staff members. Those protections make the Stone difficult to reach, but Harry and his friends eventually attempt to pass them to prevent Voldemort from getting it.", "source_chapter": [5, 13, 16], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How does Hermione's logic help the trio progress through the final obstacles?", "keywords": ["Hermione", "logic", "potions"], "reference_answer": "Hermione uses careful reasoning to solve the potion puzzle, identifying which bottle allows Harry to proceed and which one enables a person to return. Her analytical skill complements Ron's sacrifice in the chess game and Harry's willingness to continue alone.", "source_chapter": 16, "category": "multi_hop"}
{"question": "How does Ron's sacrifice in the chess game change Harry and Hermione's path?", "keywords": ["Ron", "chess", "sacrifice", "path"], "reference_answer": "Ron sacrifices himself as a chess piece so that Harry can win the game and continue toward the Stone. His decision separates Harry from his friends and makes Harry face the remaining danger on his own.", "source_chapter": 16, "category": "multi_hop"}
{"question": "How do the Dursleys' efforts to prevent Harry's letters lead to the meeting with Hagrid?", "keywords": ["letters", "Dursleys", "Hagrid", "Hogwarts"], "reference_answer": "Vernon repeatedly destroys or evades Hogwarts letters and eventually takes the family away to a remote hut. Hagrid finds them there on Harry's eleventh birthday and gives Harry the letter and the truth about his identity.", "source_chapter": [3, 4], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How do Harry's early unexplained experiences foreshadow his identity as a wizard?", "keywords": ["magic", "hair", "zoo"], "reference_answer": "Harry's hair grows back overnight, a sweater shrinks when Petunia tries to force it on him, and the glass at the zoo disappears when he is speaking to a snake. These events foreshadow that Harry can perform magic even before he understands it.", "source_chapter": [2, 4], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How do the Stone's powers connect to Voldemort's goal and the ending?", "keywords": ["Stone", "Elixir of Life", "Voldemort", "ending"], "reference_answer": "The Stone can produce the Elixir of Life, which grants continued life, and can turn metal into gold. Voldemort wants it to restore or sustain himself, but Harry prevents him from obtaining it and Dumbledore later arranges for the Stone to be destroyed.", "source_chapter": [13, 17], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How does the story link the scar, the attack on the Potters, and Voldemort's failure to kill Harry?", "keywords": ["scar", "Potters", "Voldemort"], "reference_answer": "Harry's lightning-shaped scar comes from Voldemort's attempt to kill him as a baby. Voldemort's power breaks when the curse fails to kill Harry, and the scar remains as a physical sign of the attack and their connection.", "source_chapter": [1, 17], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How does the trio's rule-breaking serve both their friendship and the larger plot?", "keywords": ["friendship", "Stone"], "reference_answer": "Harry, Ron, and Hermione break school rules because they believe the Stone is in danger. Their shared investigation strengthens their friendship, but their nighttime activities also expose them to danger and result in points being deducted from Gryffindor before the end-of-year awards.", "source_chapter": [9, 13, 15, 16], "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How do the forest detention and the discovery of the hooded figure connect Harry to the threat surrounding the Stone?", "keywords": ["Forbidden Forest", "detention", "unicorn", "hooded figure"], "reference_answer": "During detention, Harry sees a hooded figure drinking unicorn blood. Firenze explains the terrible cost of drinking it and suggests the figure is seeking survival, linking the forest encounter to the danger posed by Voldemort and his pursuit of the Stone.", "source_chapter": 15, "category": "multi_hop"}
# {"question": "How does the final confrontation resolve both the immediate danger and Harry's understanding of his past?", "keywords": ["Quirrell", "Voldemort", "parents"], "reference_answer": "Harry stops Quirrell from taking the Stone, and Quirrell dies when he cannot withstand Harry's protective power. Dumbledore then explains that Lily's sacrifice protected Harry, connecting the immediate victory to the tragedy of his parents' deaths.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "multi_hop"}
{"question": "What is the sequence of events that leads from Voldemort's disappearance to Harry being left at Privet Drive?", "keywords": ["Voldemort", "Potters", "Hagrid", "Privet Drive"], "reference_answer": "Voldemort attacks the Potters and fails to kill Harry; his power collapses. Hagrid retrieves Harry from the damaged house using Sirius Black's motorcycle, and Dumbledore leaves Harry with a letter on the Dursleys' doorstep.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "chronology"}
# {"question": "What happens after Harry receives his first Hogwarts letter?", "keywords": ["Vernon", "letters", "Hagrid"], "reference_answer": "Vernon takes the letter and refuses to let Harry read it. More letters arrive, Vernon destroys them and tries to prevent delivery, then he takes the family away until Hagrid finds Harry and explains the truth.", "source_chapter": [3, 4], "category": "chronology"}
{"question": "In what order does Harry meet Ron and Hermione and become friends with them?", "keywords": ["Ron", "Hermione", "Hogwarts Express", "troll"], "reference_answer": "Harry meets Ron on the Hogwarts Express and they share food and conversation. He encounters Hermione during the train journey and at school, but the three become close friends after Harry and Ron rescue her from a troll.", "source_chapter": [6, 7, 10], "category": "chronology"}
{"question": "What sequence leads to Harry joining the Gryffindor Quidditch team?", "keywords": ["flying", "McGonagall", "Wood", "Seeker"], "reference_answer": "Harry catches Neville's Remembrall while flying, impressing McGonagall. She takes him to Oliver Wood, introduces him as a potential Seeker, and arranges for him to join the team.", "source_chapter": 9, "category": "chronology"}
{"question": "How does the trio's investigation of the Stone progress from suspicion to action?", "keywords": ["Stone", "Fluffy", "Snape", "investigation"], "reference_answer": "They learn that a dangerous object is being guarded at Hogwarts, suspect Snape after the Quidditch incident, and investigate the forbidden corridor and the three-headed dog. Their suspicion grows as they gather clues, eventually leading them to go after the Stone themselves.", "source_chapter": [9, 11, 13, 16], "category": "chronology"}
# {"question": "What happens to Norbert from the time Hagrid obtains the egg until the dragon leaves?", "keywords": ["Norbert", "dragon egg", "Charlie"], "reference_answer": "Hagrid obtains a dragon egg and secretly raises Norbert. The dragon grows quickly and becomes difficult to hide, so Harry and Hermione arrange for Ron's brother Charlie and his friends to take it away by broomstick.", "source_chapter": [14, 15], "category": "chronology"}
# {"question": "What happens during Harry's first encounter with the Mirror of Erised, and what follows?", "keywords": ["Mirror of Erised", "parents", "Dumbledore"], "reference_answer": "Harry finds the mirror at night and sees his parents and other relatives in it. He returns repeatedly, becoming absorbed in what it shows, until Dumbledore finds him and explains that the mirror reveals a person's deepest desire and can become dangerous if it replaces living.", "source_chapter": 12, "category": "chronology"}
{"question": "What sequence brings Harry, Ron, and Hermione to the final protected area?", "keywords": ["chess", "Devil's Snare", "keys", "potions"], "reference_answer": "They pass through the Devil's Snare, use their skills to catch a flying key, and cross the enchanted chessboard, where Ron sacrifices himself. Harry and Hermione continue through the remaining challenges, including the potions puzzle, before Harry goes on alone.", "source_chapter": 16, "category": "chronology"}
# {"question": "What happens in the Forbidden Forest during Harry's detention?", "keywords": ["detention", "Forbidden Forest", "unicorn", "Firenze"], "reference_answer": "Harry, Hermione, Neville, and Malfoy accompany Hagrid into the Forbidden Forest. Harry and Malfoy follow a trail and find a hooded figure drinking unicorn blood; Firenze intervenes and carries Harry away from the danger.", "source_chapter": 15, "category": "chronology"}
# {"question": "How does the confrontation with Quirrell unfold?", "keywords": ["Quirrell", "Stone", "Voldemort"], "reference_answer": "Harry reaches the final chamber and finds Quirrell rather than Snape. Quirrell reveals Voldemort, attempts to take the Stone from Harry, and is burned by Harry's touch; Harry then loses consciousness and later wakes in the hospital wing.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "chronology"}
# {"question": "How does the school year end after Harry's encounter with Quirrell?", "keywords": ["hospital wing", "points", "Gryffindor"], "reference_answer": "Harry recovers in the hospital wing and speaks with Dumbledore, who explains the Stone and Harry's protection. At the end-of-year feast, Dumbledore awards points for the trio's courage and Neville's attempt to stand up to his friends, helping Gryffindor win the House Cup.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "chronology"}
# {"question": "What happens between Harry's arrival at Hogwarts and his first Quidditch match?", "keywords": ["arrival", "sorting", "lessons", "Quidditch"], "reference_answer": "Harry is sorted into Gryffindor, settles into school, makes friends, learns about the wizarding world, and demonstrates remarkable flying ability. McGonagall arranges for him to become Seeker, and he trains before playing his first match.", "source_chapter": [7, 8, 9, 11], "category": "chronology"}
{"question": "What small detail reveals that Harry's letter is meant specifically for him?", "keywords": ["letter", "address", "cupboard", "Privet Drive"], "reference_answer": "It is addressed to Harry Potter at the cupboard under the stairs at number four, Privet Drive, Little Whinging, Surrey, even though the Dursleys have tried to keep his wizarding identity hidden.", "source_chapter": 3, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
{"question": "How many times does Dumbledore click the Put-Outer to extinguish the street lamps?", "keywords": ["Put-Outer", "street lamps", "Dumbledore"], "reference_answer": "He clicks it twelve times, removing the street's lights.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What does the Hogwarts letter's seal depict?", "keywords": ["letter", "seal", "coat of arms"], "reference_answer": "The purple wax seal bears a coat of arms with a lion, an eagle, a badger, and a snake around a large letter H.", "source_chapter": 3, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
{"question": "What is unusual about Dumbledore's watch?", "keywords": ["Dumbledore", "watch", "hands", "planets"], "reference_answer": "It has twelve hands but no numbers; little planets move around its edge.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What is the name of the three-headed dog guarding a trapdoor?", "keywords": ["three-headed dog", "trapdoor", "Fluffy"], "reference_answer": "The dog is called Fluffy.", "source_chapter": 9, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What does Harry see when he looks into the Mirror of Erised?", "keywords": ["Mirror of Erised", "parents", "family"], "reference_answer": "He sees himself surrounded by his parents and other family members, whom he has never known.", "source_chapter": 12, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What item does Hagrid give Harry that helps him learn about his parents and the wizarding world?", "keywords": ["Hagrid", "gift"], "reference_answer": "Hagrid gives Harry a photo album containing moving photographs of his parents and other people from their lives.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What is the name of the dragon Hagrid raises?", "keywords": ["dragon", "name", "Norbert"], "reference_answer": "Hagrid names the dragon Norbert.", "source_chapter": 14, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "Which animal does Harry speak to at the zoo?", "keywords": ["zoo", "animal", "snake"], "reference_answer": "He speaks to a boa constrictor from Brazil.", "source_chapter": 2, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What is the name of the wizarding bank?", "keywords": ["bank", "Gringotts"], "reference_answer": "The bank is Gringotts Wizarding Bank.", "source_chapter": 5, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What does Hagrid use to transport baby Harry to Privet Drive?", "keywords": ["Hagrid", "motorcycle", "Sirius Black"], "reference_answer": "He uses a flying motorcycle lent to him by Sirius Black.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What is the name of the game Harry plays for Gryffindor?", "keywords": ["sport", "Quidditch", "Gryffindor"], "reference_answer": "He plays Quidditch, serving as Gryffindor's Seeker.", "source_chapter": 11, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What kind of sweet does Dumbledore offer McGonagall?", "keywords": ["Dumbledore", "McGonagall", "sweet"], "reference_answer": "He offers her a lemon drop.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
{"question": "What does Hagrid accidentally reveal that leads Harry to investigate the Stone?", "keywords": ["Hagrid", "Fluffy", "Stone", "secret"], "reference_answer": "Hagrid reveals that Fluffy is guarding something connected to the object removed from Gringotts, and his mention of the dog and its music-related weakness gives Harry and his friends a clue about the hidden protections.", "source_chapter": [9, 13], "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
# {"question": "What object does Harry catch during his first notable flying incident?", "keywords": ["flying", "Neville", "Remembrall"], "reference_answer": "He catches Neville's Remembrall after it is dropped and flies through the air to retrieve it.", "source_chapter": 9, "category": "fine_grained_detail"}
{"question": "How does Harry's first-year experience change from his life at Privet Drive?", "keywords": ["Privet Drive", "Hogwarts", "change", "belonging"], "reference_answer": "At Privet Drive, Harry is neglected and treated as an unwanted burden. At Hogwarts, he finds friendship, discovers his abilities, learns about his parents, and becomes part of a community where he matters.", "source_chapter": [2, 5, 7, 17], "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "What does the story suggest through the contrast between the Dursleys' house and Hogwarts?", "keywords": ["Dursleys", "Hogwarts", "home"], "reference_answer": "The contrast suggests that a true home is shaped by acceptance and belonging rather than by appearances or material comfort. The Dursleys' tidy house excludes Harry, while Hogwarts gives him friendship and a place in the world.", "source_chapter": [1, 2, 5, 7], "category": "paraphrase"}
{"question": "How would you paraphrase Dumbledore's concern that fame could affect Harry?", "keywords": ["Dumbledore", "fame", "childhood", "protection"], "reference_answer": "Dumbledore believes that growing up celebrated for surviving Voldemort would burden Harry before he is old enough to understand it, so he wants Harry to have an ordinary childhood for as long as possible.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "paraphrase"}
{"question": "What does the Mirror of Erised reveal about Harry beyond his curiosity?", "keywords": ["Mirror of Erised", "desire", "parents", "loss"], "reference_answer": "It reveals Harry's profound longing for the family and affection he has been denied, rather than simply a desire for adventure or recognition.", "source_chapter": 12, "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "How can Harry's friendship with Ron and Hermione be described in terms of their different abilities?", "keywords": ["friendship", "Ron", "Hermione"], "reference_answer": "Their friendship works because they contribute different strengths. Harry is brave and persistent, Ron understands wizarding customs and strategy, and Hermione is scholarly and logical.", "source_chapter": [6, 7, 16], "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "What does Harry's reaction to the wizarding world show about his life with the Dursleys?", "keywords": ["Dursleys", "discovery", "belonging"], "reference_answer": "His excitement at discovering magic shows how restricted and lonely his previous life has been. The wizarding world offers explanations, friendships, and possibilities that the Dursleys deliberately kept from him.", "source_chapter": [2, 4, 5], "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "How does the novel present Snape's role in the mystery?", "keywords": ["Snape", "mystery"], "reference_answer": "Snape is presented as a threatening and suspicious figure, encouraging Harry to believe he is after the Stone. That impression is a misdirection, since Quirrell is the person helping Voldemort.", "source_chapter": [8, 11, 13, 17], "category": "paraphrase"}
{"question": "What does Neville's final act of opposition to his friends mean?", "keywords": ["Neville", "courage", "friends", "House Cup"], "reference_answer": "Neville's decision to challenge Harry, Ron, and Hermione shows that courage can mean standing up to friends when one believes they are doing wrong. Dumbledore recognizes that bravery by awarding him points.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "Why is the Philosopher's Stone more than just a valuable object in the story?", "keywords": ["power", "Voldemort", "choice"], "reference_answer": "The Stone represents the temptation of wealth and the desire to escape death. The characters' choices about protecting or pursuing it reveal their values, and Dumbledore ultimately treats its power as less important than wisdom and mortality.", "source_chapter": [13, 17], "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "What does Harry's survival of Quirrell's attack suggest about the power of Lily's sacrifice?", "keywords": ["Harry", "Lily", "sacrifice", "protection"], "reference_answer": "It suggests that Lily's willing sacrifice created a protection that Voldemort did not understand and could not simply overcome. Love becomes a real force with consequences in the magical world.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "How does the novel portray rule-breaking by Harry and his friends?", "keywords": ["rules", "friendship", "courage"], "reference_answer": "Their rule-breaking is not presented as simple mischief: they believe they are preventing a serious threat. Yet their choices still have consequences, and the story distinguishes reckless behavior from courage guided by concern for others.", "source_chapter": [13, 16, 17], "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "What does the ending communicate about the meaning of being brave?", "keywords": ["ending", "bravery", "Gryffindor", "Neville"], "reference_answer": "Bravery includes acting despite fear, protecting others, and sometimes resisting one's own friends. Harry's actions matter, but Neville's courage is also recognized, showing that heroism can take different forms.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "paraphrase"}
# {"question": "How does Harry's neglect shape his response to friendship and kindness?", "keywords": ["friendship", "Harry"], "reference_answer": "Because Harry has grown up isolated and deprived of affection, ordinary kindness from Hagrid, Ron, the Weasleys, and others carries special weight. Their acceptance helps him trust people and feel that he belongs somewhere.", "source_chapter": [2, 4, 5, 6], "category": "thematic_analysis"}
# {"question": "What role does prejudice play in the Dursleys' treatment of Harry and the wizarding world?", "keywords": ["Dursleys", "magic", "difference"], "reference_answer": "The Dursleys fear anything they consider abnormal and try to suppress Harry's difference rather than understand it. Their treatment of him shows how prejudice can become cruelty and exclusion.", "source_chapter": [1, 2, 3], "category": "thematic_analysis"}
{"question": "How does the theme of belonging develop over Harry's first year?", "keywords": ["belonging", "Hogwarts", "friendship", "family"], "reference_answer": "Harry begins as an isolated child who is hidden and mistreated by the Dursleys. At Hogwarts he finds friends, a house, a team, and adults who care about him, gradually gaining a sense of belonging.", "source_chapter": [2, 5, 6, 7, 17], "category": "thematic_analysis"}
# {"question": "How does the novel contrast appearance with reality?", "keywords": ["appearance", "Snape", "Quirrell"], "reference_answer": "Harry assumes Snape's suspicious behavior means Snape is the villain, while the seemingly timid Quirrell is actually helping Voldemort. The reversal shows that outward demeanor can mislead people about a person's true intentions.", "source_chapter": [11, 13, 17], "category": "thematic_analysis"}
# {"question": "What does the Mirror of Erised suggest about desire and self-knowledge?", "keywords": ["Mirror of Erised", "desire"], "reference_answer": "The mirror reveals what a person most deeply wants, but that desire can become a trap if the person prefers the vision to real life. Harry's experience illustrates both the comfort and the danger of longing for what cannot be restored.", "source_chapter": 12, "category": "thematic_analysis"}
{"question": "How does the story connect love with protection?", "keywords": ["love", "protection", "Lily", "Harry"], "reference_answer": "Lily's sacrifice protects Harry from Voldemort, and Dumbledore explains that this protection is why Quirrell cannot bear to touch him. Love is portrayed as a force that has magical and moral power beyond physical strength.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "thematic_analysis"}
# {"question": "What does the trio's cooperation suggest about the value of different kinds of intelligence?", "keywords": ["Ron", "Hermione", "Harry"], "reference_answer": "The trio succeeds because courage, practical knowledge, logic, and strategy work together. No one character possesses every skill needed to pass the obstacles, so cooperation is more effective than individual brilliance alone.", "source_chapter": 16, "category": "thematic_analysis"}
{"question": "How does the story explore courage through Harry, Hermione, Ron, and Neville?", "keywords": ["courage", "Harry", "Hermione", "Ron", "Neville"], "reference_answer": "Courage appears as facing a dangerous mystery, standing by friends, making sacrifices, and opposing wrongdoing even when it is uncomfortable. Harry, Ron, Hermione, and Neville demonstrate these different forms at different moments.", "source_chapter": [10, 16, 17], "category": "thematic_analysis"}
# {"question": "What does Voldemort's pursuit of the Stone reveal about his values?", "keywords": ["Voldemort", "Stone", "power"], "reference_answer": "His pursuit of the Stone shows his fixation on power and continued life, even at the cost of other people. He treats the Stone as a means to overcome mortality rather than as a responsibility or a danger.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "thematic_analysis"}
{"question": "How does the novel challenge the idea that fame makes someone powerful or happy?", "keywords": ["fame", "Harry", "Dumbledore", "happiness"], "reference_answer": "Harry is famous in the wizarding world before he can understand why, but fame does not give him a happy childhood or knowledge of his parents. Dumbledore deliberately keeps him away from public adoration so he can grow up more normally.", "source_chapter": 1, "category": "thematic_analysis"}
# {"question": "What is the significance of the House Cup ending?", "keywords": ["House Cup", "Gryffindor", "Neville"], "reference_answer": "The final points reward courage and moral action, including Neville's willingness to stand up to his friends. The result shifts recognition away from mere rule-following or popularity and toward choices made for meaningful reasons.", "source_chapter": 17, "category": "thematic_analysis"}
# {"question": "How does Harry's first year show that family can mean more than biological relation?", "keywords": ["family", "Dursleys", "Weasleys", "Hagrid"], "reference_answer": "Although the Dursleys are Harry's biological relatives, they deny him care and belonging. The Weasleys, Hagrid, and other Hogwarts allies offer warmth and support, showing that meaningful family bonds also grow through kindness and acceptance.", "source_chapter": [2, 4, 6, 12], "category": "thematic_analysis"}
